\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\taxon{Example}
\title{One ordinary cube face turn}

\p{Realize the cube as #{[-1,1]^3} and let #{H} be the facet #{z=1}.
The \vocabk{facet move}{frap-000L} construction of
\citet{Definitions 5--6}{marchetti2025rubik} gives the following explicit
outer-face action. The shape rotation}
##{q(x,y,z)=(y,-x,z)}
\p{is a clockwise quarter-turn when viewed from positive #{z}, with
#{x} rightward and #{y} upward. Its restriction to #{H} supplies our move.
The corner #{(1,1,1)} goes to #{(1,-1,1)}, and its color on #{y=1}
goes to the color position on #{x=1}. The corresponding corner at
#{z=-1} is fixed by the puzzle move, although the global map #{q} moves it.}

\tikzfig2{\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
\node (a) at (-1.4,1.4) {$(-,+)$};
\node (b) at (1.4,1.4) {$(+,+)$};
\node (c) at (1.4,-1.4) {$(+,-)$};
\node (d) at (-1.4,-1.4) {$(-,-)$};
\draw[->] (a) -- (b);
\draw[->] (b) -- (c);
\draw[->] (c) -- (d);
\draw[->] (d) -- (a);
\node at (0,0) {$(H,H)$};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The signs give the #{x,y} coordinates of the four corners on #{z=1}.
The arrows show their clockwise motion; the central incidence record
#{(H,H)} stays fixed. No orientation of a marked center is recorded.}

\p{Restricting the second entry to facets gives
#{8\cdot3+12\cdot2+6=54} incidence positions: three per corner,
two per edge and one per face center. The turn moves twelve corner
stickers and eight edge stickers, fixing the other thirty-four positions,
including the unmarked center of the turned face. This is the usual
outer-face action on visible stickers of a #{3\times3\times3} cube in
a fixed frame.}

\p{The full incidence set has #{8\cdot7+12\cdot3+6=98} elements:
above each vertex are that vertex, three edges and three facets;
above each edge are itself and two facets. The facet-colored subset is
invariant under every move. Its restricted action supplies the comparison
with ordinary stickers; a faithful restriction for all abstract polytopes
is not needed. Marked center orientations and inner-layer turns are
additional data, absent from this comparison.}
